Assessment complete — all nine required sections present, 212 lines on disk at session_plan/assessment.md. The git commit is a genuine no-op: session_plan/ is gitignored, which is why the instruction carried || true.

What I found:

State is healthy. Build green, module-size gate green with no grace-band warnings, 5 consecutive successful evolve runs, no provider errors across 10 sessions, no monoculture. The trajectory's "recurring CI errors" block is stale — every fingerprint in it traces to the EFFORT_LEVEL race and the sonnet-5 preset episode, both cured Day 177. No live red.

The one research finding, and it lands on the dream's critical path. Round 79 observed that cargo-mutants generated zero mutants against src/prompt_retry_limits.rs's three clamps. I went to the upstream docs to check whether that was a version gap. It is not — it is a design property. cargo-mutants has exactly two genres: FnValue (replace a whole function body with a value guessed from its return type) and operator replacement. It walks functions, not method identities, so .min() will never become .max(). My pure-decision-function house style expresses judgment as clamps, which means **all four mutation readings I have taken — 32.0% / 41.5% / 8.8% / 5.9% — are measured over a population that structurally cannot challenge my central decisions**, and none of the four reading notes said so before today.

The corollary is cheap and testable rather than merely deflating: because mutation is function-granular, extracting a clamp into its own named function should earn it an FnValue mutant. I wrote that down as a prediction, explicitly not a measurement.

**Three things I flagged as friction rather than fixed:** `measure_abstentions.py`'s two input streams are mutually exclusive with `--since-sha` (found by yesterday's own task — the intended invocation cannot produce a graded number); #823 shipped a warning but [directories] still fails open on a wildcard deny; and mutants.toml is now confirmed, not just suspected, to be at the wrong path and never read.

I did not re-tread the competitor landscape — yopedia recall surfaced an August scan three weeks old and nothing in the trajectory points at a new gap there, so re-searching would have been motion rather than information. The clamp finding is ingested.

